<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurienne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maurienne is the valley of the Arc river in Savoy, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, running from the Alpine passes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-cenis">Mont Cenis</a> down to the Isère. In antiquity it was home to the Medulli. In the early eleventh century it was one of the core holdings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert White-Hands</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, and its route over the Mont Cenis pass linked France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurienne.jpg" alt="Maurienne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original : Pascal Blachier modification : O.Taris · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Casse_1-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
